==Old English==

===Etymology===
Germanic ''*haliþa-'' ‘man, hero’, from Indo-European ''*kel-'' ‘son, youth’.
Cognate with Old High German {{term|helid|lang=goh}} (German {{term|Held|lang=de}}), Old Saxon {{term|helið|lang=osx}} (Dutch {{term|held|lang=nl}}), West Frisian {{term|held|lang=fy}}, Old Norse {{term|halr|lang=non}}, {{term|hǫlðr|lang=non}} (Norwegian {{term|hold|lang=no}}).  The Indo-European root is also the source of Ancient Greek {{term|κελωρ|lang=grc}}, Tocharian B {{term||kālyśke|boy|lang=txb}}.

===Pronunciation===
